lebanon's hezbollah and its allies look  set to lose their parliamentary majority  that's according to preliminary results  from the first general election since  the country's economic collapse  voters took to the streets to celebrate  with supporters of opposition candidates  saying they hope to deal a blow to the  ruling politicians who they blame for  the crisis  turnout for the elections was low  our reporter rebecca ritters is in  beirut with the hezbollah bloc likely to  lose that majority what will the new  lebanese government look like rebecca  well but it's still a little bit too  early to be able to make any predictions  as you said yourself votes are still  trickling in but even after the votes  are all completely counted lebanon takes  is notorious for taking a very long time  to form governments so exactly how it  will look uh is hard to say at this  point but as you mentioned hezbollah  does look to be losing its parliamentary  majority that instead going to the  far-right christian lebanese forces uh  part of the so-called saudi us alliance  and they will be able to there may make  up an alliance a coalition government  they'll have to nominate a prime  minister who will then be able to form a  cabinet now that could take months so  lebanon has been known to be without a  government for two years at a time as  the sort of infighting and faction  behind behind the scenes faction  fighting goes on but what is clear and  what was really going into this election  one of the huge issues was the hezbollah  issue and it does appear that people  have voted with their feet many going  into this election saying hezbollah has  caused all the problems other people  saying that hezbollah isn't the only  problem in this country and we need to  deal with corruption on all levels but  people certainly voting with their feet  a lot of independent candidates also  winning seats we're looking at maybe 10  seats for the so-called change  opposition candidates and they'll play  an important role as you've got these  two opposing blocks the lebanese forces  on one side hezbollah on the other and  these independent candidates playing  quite an important role in the middle  okay whatever sort of coalition we get  and however long it takes what are  people expecting from the new government  because i bet the list is long  very long indeed uh the one word that  really could sum up going into this vote  was changed people really want to see a  change in this country it was the first  vote uh since the country's near  complete economic collapse and 80  percent of people in this country now  live below the poverty line the currency  has lost 90 percent of its value  the electricity is on for one hour a day  people have to survive with generators  but they can't afford fuel to run them  so people really living day to day and  they want change they're really fed up  most of the people i've spoken to said  uh you know that they weren't going to  vote for establishment parties now  obviously there are other people and  that's why the lebanese forces have come  out in front who believe that lebanese  forces were the only way to make change  because it was going to get hezbollah  out other people saying that isn't the  case but that is definitely what people  are calling for and it is does seem to  have been heard and heeded and you know  i think that the the new government  whichever form it takes is going to have  to listen to that  uh in the future of lebanese politics  rebecca why then such a poor turnout at  the polling booth  yeah that was quite surprising actually  and really disappointing here a lot of  people yesterday saying we just can't  believe it we were expecting such a high  turnout and especially with the diaspora  vote which was one week earlier and that  had a surprisingly high turnout with a  lot of people voting outside of lebanon  a lot more than in previous years but 41  of people came to the polls yesterday  and that's much lower than even the last  election which was nearly 50 so people  very disappointed uh there's a lot of  reasons one being that uh the sunni sect  uh really didn't have a candidate and  then they said that they were going to  sadhari who stepped out of lebanese  politics a few months ago  they said that they um were not going to  vote and of course you've got voter  apathy people saying that it's too  little too late we can't really make any  change there's many reasons for that low  voter turnout ben rebecca readers  foreign thank you
 

